<p>1. The reform timetable<br>
Several of the reforms announced last year are now in force, and more are
due in 2027. Our updated timeline sets out each change, when it takes
effect and what employers should do first. Most employers will save time by
reviewing contracts, policies and manager training together rather than one
change at a time.</p>
<p>2. Flexible working<br>
Employers can still refuse a flexible working request for a business
reason, but under the reforms they will also need to show that the refusal
is reasonable. We look at what a well-documented decision looks like, and
the questions managers should ask before saying no.</p>
<p>3. Holiday pay and record-keeping<br>
Tribunals expect employers to keep adequate records of working time and
holiday. We set out the records to keep, for how long, and the gaps we most
often find in audits.</p>
<p>4. Preventing sexual harassment<br>
Every employer is under a duty to take reasonable steps to prevent sexual
harassment of its workers. Our short checklist covers your policy,
reporting routes and training.</p>
<p>5. Webinar<br>
Join our employment team for a one-hour review of the year, with time for
questions. Register at https://news.brightlaw.co.uk/webinar-2026</p>
